{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792593377,"nonce":"lLWfn7nI/Z+Yi10N66mAJHFfv+M=","message":"Implemented through the existing shared snapshot format (schema 13), with no TS-specific cache envelope. The runtime preparation helper resolves imports in place, strips consumed AST bodies, preserves generic templates, and reuses the shared snapshot optimizer and release facts. Prepared snapshots can execute even if atomic publication fails. Check-only cache creation remains typed-AST-only; a runtime load upgrades that entry. Full source/resolution validation includes type-only dependencies. Native primitive sources participate in the frontend build fingerprint.\n\nAn owned single-module snapshot now moves its encoded image into LazyModule instead of allocating a second copy of all HIR bodies. The shared linker also roots explicitly configured host entries located solely in a split snapshot. Warm hits keep unrelated bodies encoded and begin execution in the interpreter. Tests cover a new boolean specialization of a generic saved after numeric use, F64 GEPs, exactly-once releases along each return path, initialization ordering, unavailable persistence, validation/invalidation and actual Cranelift/LLVM execution.\n\nValidation: compiler lib+integration tests with LLVM: 881 passed, 16 ignored (serial fixtures because of the separate process-global tier callback issue); TypeScript LLVM+allocation-audit: 79 passed, 4 diagnostic tests ignored; default TS: 79 passed; embed lib plus initializer and snapshot borrowing/move tests: 101 passed. Conformance against pinned TypeScript 6.0.2 expectations: 735 uncached + 735 cache-enabled comparisons passed across interpreter, baseline and LLVM profiles. Clippy -D warnings passed for TS/embed and affected compiler targets; parser-only WASM and Python/Lua/ZynML library checks passed. Debug/release zynode builds and formatting/diff checks passed. Dedicated fresh-process LLVM initialization regression separately exercises 16 concurrent threads.\n\nFrozen release binaries on idle NUC (i5-1250P, x86_64 Linux, Rust 1.101.0-nightly c36f14571, LLVM 21.1.8), same LLVM-enabled build profile and lockfile. Candidate SHA256 b53bca21e9cb1241f687756d076b6f1d996c7ee45681328866da15932e3226a4; previous AST-cache reference 3ab5290bfb8770a519f69bc27acc82a94378d6d78b5c591d356c19dede46c17a (base f25ea49daf51faea5a9a515e5d0002ef207affc3 plus the same native-target initialization fix). Nine shuffled process samples per case/mode, 108 samples total, affinity CPUs 0-7. Before each sample: no compiler jobs, at most 0.673 busy cores, temperature \u003c=70C; no observed throttle-counter increments and hashes unchanged. These are pre-sample checks, not continuous monitoring. No Cargo ran between samples. All stdout/exit checks passed. Runtime flags --llvm --baseline-threshold 2000000000 --no-osr isolate interpreter-first startup, not LLVM throughput.\n\nMedian process wall ms (previous AST hit -\u003e new HIR hit; candidate uncached source):\n- tiny: 4.940 -\u003e 4.827; source 4.995.\n- wide 1,000 functions, only f0 called: 14.551 -\u003e 8.281 (1.76x); source 16.527.\n- 24 imported libraries with generic calls, arrays, barrel exports and a type-only dependency: 9.529 -\u003e 6.204 (1.54x); source 9.089.\n\nCold creation has a real tradeoff. Single observations (previous AST store -\u003e new HIR store): tiny 5.425 -\u003e 5.640 ms; wide 19.487 -\u003e 87.247 ms; imports 9.499 -\u003e 12.342 ms. HIR snapshot sizes tiny 6,604, wide 1,485,187, imports 227,398 bytes versus AST 3,065 / 1,039,080 / 164,455. Current cold preparation synchronously lowers and optimizes the entire open project. Separate instrumented wide run: lower_program 20.75 ms, inlining 8.42 + 7.73 ms, scalar_replace_alloc 9.41 + 0.19 ms. Trace perturbs wall time; this is diagnostic rather than another cold benchmark.\n\nDebug allocation diagnostic over load+compile on the same wide fixture: AST hit 66,636 allocations / 3,123 reallocations / 15,507,832 requested bytes; HIR hit 10,981 / 80 / 6,720,396 (~57% fewer requested bytes). Cold HIR upgrade was 713,582 / 37,414 / 143,398,983. These are allocator requests, not RSS or native-library allocations. Diagnostic debug timings are not used for the startup verdict. Local release timing batch was discarded because another Cargo/rustc build was active; NUC evidence above is the valid performance comparison.\n\nEvidence retained locally: /private/tmp/ts-prelower-nuc-final.json, /private/tmp/ts-prelower-conformance{,-cached}.json, /private/tmp/ts-prelower-{compiler-serial,ts-verified,default-ts,embed-final,allocations}.log. NUC driver/report: /home/raikage/zyntax-ts-cache-startup.py and /home/raikage/zyntax-ts-cache-startup-nuc-final.json; cold trace /home/raikage/zyntax-ts-cache-cold-phase-20261021.trace. Normal NUC checkout was left untouched; builds used isolated candidate/reference checkouts.\n\nFollow-up 48b697dc20d1db83e3cf17d097527aa61f7a9fb53f291b04e30c6f866512052c tracks reducing unused-body preparation and moving it to an owned worker, cancelled at process completion without waiting for cache generation. Threading is not implemented in this change. Separate open bug 718284cd3fe83b90248bf068576db3f97cb48dceacb33ca5c0ef2cd999cb18ec tracks process-global lazy/promotion callback ownership between simultaneous runtimes; runtime test fixtures are serialized rather than hiding that production issue with another global lock.","files":null}]}